From: Carl Andersson <carl.andersson@dynamist.se>
Subject: [PATCH] x86_64: load the initrd at a page-aligned address

The initrd ends at the top of low RAM, so it starts at that address
minus its size. An initrd whose size is not a multiple of the page size
then starts inside a page. The guest frees the initrd page by page and
warns on every boot (arch/x86/mm/init.c, free_init_pages), which taints
the kernel and hides the next real warning. Firecracker rounds the
address down to a page for the same reason.
---
--- a/src/arch/src/x86_64/mod.rs
+++ b/src/arch/src/x86_64/mod.rs
@@ -162,7 +162,7 @@
         // To be filled later using GuestMemory.
         guest_last_addr: 0,
         page_size,
-        initrd_addr: ram_last_addr.min(MMIO_MEM_START) - initrd_size,
+        initrd_addr: (ram_last_addr.min(MMIO_MEM_START) - initrd_size) & !(page_size as u64 - 1),
         firmware_addr,
     };
     (info, regions)
